3. DEBUG SEARCH INPUT CHANGES
================================================================================

Add logging inside handleSearchChange():

const handleSearchChange = (e) => {

  const newValue = e.target.value;

  console.log('Old searchTerm:', searchTerm);
  console.log('New searchTerm:', newValue);

  setSearchTerm(newValue);

};


Expected Output:

Old searchTerm: javascript
New searchTerm: react


This verifies:

- onChange is firing
- Input value is available
- setSearchTerm() is being called


================================================================================
4. IMPORTANT: REACT STATE UPDATES
================================================================================

React state updates are scheduled.

Example:

const handleSearchChange = (e) => {

  const newValue = e.target.value;

  setSearchTerm(newValue);

  console.log(searchTerm);

};


Do not expect the log immediately after setSearchTerm() to always show
the newly requested state value in the same handler.

To inspect the new input value immediately, use:

console.log(newValue);


To observe committed searchTerm changes:

useEffect(() => {

  console.log(
    'searchTerm updated:',
    searchTerm
  );

}, [searchTerm]);


